WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.370
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.550
X-Men Apocalypse's day of reckoning has finally
arrived and I've been able to hunt down all

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.719
the (best) easter eggs and references from
the latest X-Men movie.

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:17.140
Of course, there will be spoilers ahead, so
if you want to avoid any spoilers, click here

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:21.039
for my spoiler-free review or come back after
you've seen the movie.

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:24.470
And as promised, I'm giving away the first
of two packs of official X-Men Apocalypse

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.109
merch on this video.

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:30.810
To enter the giveway you just need to subscribe
and leave a comment or reply to a comment

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.220
about your favourite easter egg in the movie.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:38.120
One of the movie's best moments is when Hugh
Jackman's Wolverine emerges from his Weapon

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.379
X cell in full-on berserker mode.

00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:44.809
The headgear and cables, belt and gadgets
around his waist, and the spikes in his torso

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:49.520
are a reference to his original training gear
from the Weapon X comics, written and illustrated

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:50.520
by Barry Windsor-Smith.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:55.180
And the red light we see on his helmet is
nod to the red visor on his virtual reality

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:56.809
helmet in the comics.

00:00:56.809 --> 00:01:01.269
The location where this all takes place is
Alkali Lake, which we saw in the first X-Men

00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:05.920
trilogy and in X-Men Origins: Wolverine, although
with the changed timeline since Days of Future

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.220
Past, the Wolverine Weapon X we see here is
a new version.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:14.000
And the moment where Jean Grey touches Wolverine's
head, soothes him, and restores a part of

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.390
his memory is a nod to the relationship between
Wolverine and Famke Janssen's Jean Grey in

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:23.170
the original X-Men trilogy, even if here it
feels rather age-inappropriate.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.040
It's also an interesting twist on The Last
Stand where it was Logan who tried to soothe

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.540
Jean Grey from her dark Phoenix self.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.500
And there are lots of hints that the Dark
Phoenix saga will be revisited in the X-Men

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.479
movie universe, hopefully in a more satisfying
way than it was previously.

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:41.979
When Jean wakes up from her nightmare, the
talk between her and Charles about a dark

00:01:41.979 --> 00:01:46.260
power inside her together with visions of
flames is an indication of what's to come,

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.469
and her words that "I'm afraid someday I'm
going to hurt someone" are also a nod to how

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:53.149
she killed Scott Summers in The Last Stand.

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:57.710
In the final showdown Jean learns to harness
the powerful Phoenix energy when Charles pleads

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:00.600
with her to unleash it so they can defeat
Apocalypse.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.821
And the moment is completed with the image
of a burning bird around Jean, with Apocalypse

00:02:04.821 --> 00:02:07.049
proclaiming "All is revealed!"

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:12.390
Thanks to the popularity of Quicksilver's
amazing frozen time sequence in Days of Future

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:17.550
Past, Evan Peters gets not just one, but two
fantastic slow-mo scenes in X-Men: Apocalypse.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:21.980
And the pizza dog that Quicksilver rescues
is an easter egg from the comics where Hawkeye

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.330
recues a dog called Lucky aka Pizza Dog.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:30.410
And when Quicksilver surfs out of the exploding
X-Mansion on a table, it's a cool visual nod

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:35.560
to the Silver Surfer from the comics and of
course the Fantastic Four 2007 sequel Rise

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.610
of the Silver Surfer.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.911
There's also a wink to the X-Men-themed V
Energy Drink ad that Evan Peters did when

00:02:41.911 --> 00:02:46.280
Quicksilver stops to take a sip of the TAB
soda can from a girl holding the drink during

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.570
the X-Mansion explosion.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:52.920
By the way, after the hints from Days of Future
Past, we also get an amusing confirmation

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.850
that Magneto is Quicksilver's father, as per
the comics.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:01.710
The Six Million Dollar Man T-shirt that Quicksilver
wears is a wink to Apocalypse director Bryan

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:05.770
Singer's rumoured plans to make a big-screen
version of the 70s TV show The Six Million

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:06.940
Dollar Man.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:12.090
And Quicksilver's room has some cool pop culture
references from the 80s such as a Ms Pac-Man

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:16.260
arcade game and the Knight Rider theme was
playing on his TV during the scene in his

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.260
room.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:20.620
Blob from X-Men Origins: Wolverine makes a
brief appearance as the opponent Angel knocks

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.910
out before Nightcrawler is brought in to the
mutant cage fight.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:28.140
This scene is also a callback to our first
introduction to Wolverine in the very first

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.980
X-Men movie when he's cage-fighting in a bar.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:36.370
By the way, in X-Men Origins: Wolverine, it
was heavily implied Blob was killed, but in

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:40.290
this movie's new timeline, Blob, although
unconscious, seems to be alive.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:45.540
There's a reference to Nightcrawler's comic
book and X-Men 2 origins in the circus when

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.370
he's introduced at the cage fight.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:52.240
The fact that it's Raven aka Mystique who
rescues Nightcrawler in East Berlin is a shout-out

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:56.040
to their history in the comic books where
Mystique is actually Nightcrawler's mother

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.950
and his father is Azazel.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:03.480
When the cage fight announcer introduces Angel
as the "Angel of Death", it not only foreshadows

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.530
his becoming Apocalypse's horseman "Death"
in this film, but it's also a reference to

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.760
the same thing happening in the comics.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.460
In the movie, like in the comics, Apocalypse
also transforms Angel into Archangel pushing

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:18.310
him through a painful process of shedding
his old feather wings and growing new metal

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.540
ones that can shoot metal feathers like knives.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:24.850
By the way, the track playing during this
scene is, appropriately enough, heavy metal

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:28.000
band Metallica's 'The Four Horsemen'.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.400
The underground business that the mutant Caliban
runs in the movie is a nod to the time he

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.180
spent in the comics with the underground mutant
community the Morlocks.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:40.020
In the comics, Caliban has the power to sense
and track where other mutants are within a

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:45.080
certain distance and in the film his job is
to track down mutants for the right price.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:49.461
The comic book Caliban becomes Apocalypse's
horseman "Pestilence", but in the movie, it's

00:04:49.461 --> 00:04:53.250
his henchwoman Psylocke who takes on this
role.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.180
Psylocke has a rather limited role in X-Men:
Apocalypse, but she does get a number of nods

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.310
to her comic book characteristics.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:04.470
She gets a comic book faithful, albeit impractical,
costume and a pair of pink telekinetic katanas

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:09.820
which she can slice through just about anything
with including, as we see in this movie, a

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:10.820
car.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:14.750
Magneto's story of trying to live with his
family among ordinary people in Eastern Europe

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:18.940
is based on a similar storyline from the X-Men
comics, where he lives with a Romani woman

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:19.950
called Magda.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.170
In the comics, they have a daughter called
Anya, rather than Nina, as she's called in

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:24.280
the movie.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:28.210
Similar to the film, tragedy befalls Magneto
when his daughter dies in a fire.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.400
When police prevent Magneto from going in
to save Anya, he lashes out and kills them

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.340
all, as he does in the movie.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:38.611
Comic book Magneto also loses his wife Magda
when she flees afraid of what he's become.

00:05:38.611 --> 00:05:44.300
By the way, in the comics Magda later gives
birth to Magneto's children Wanda and Pietro,

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.780
aka Scarlet Witch and Quicksilver.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:51.190
In the movie, during his time in Poland, Magneto
takes on the alias Henryk, which is a nod

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:54.890
to when he changed his name in the comics
to Eric Lensherr, which is, of course, the

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:58.100
name he goes by in the X-Men movie universe.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:02.070
Magneto's daughter Nina's ability to communicate
telepathically with animals such as the deer

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:06.139
we see by the house or the birds she uses
to attack the police is a reference to the

00:06:06.139 --> 00:06:11.389
comic-book mutant Lin Li, aka Nature Girl,
who has antlers on her head and who can bond

00:06:11.389 --> 00:06:14.480
with and control animals.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:18.750
At the end of the movie, Cyclops gets a new
visor and an iconic costume complete with

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:22.740
shoulder straps similar to the Jim Lee design
in the 1990s comics.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.830
The argument the school jock has with Scott
about having his eyes on his girl is a little

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:31.870
inside joke to the rivalry between James Marsden's
Cyclops and Wolverine over Scott's girlfriend

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.480
Jean Grey in the original X-Men movie trilogy.

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:39.560
And the jacket Scott Summer's bully wears
has the words Wedgewood Pirates on the back,

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:44.920
possibly a wink to the space pirate Corsair
who's Scott's father in the comics, especially

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:51.110
as Scott says his bully looks old enough to
be the father of their female classmate.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:56.740
Storm's mohawk matches the hairstyle makeover
she got in the 1983 comic Uncanny X-Men No.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:57.740
173.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:02.830
And her pickpocketing skills also have their
origins in the comics, where an orphaned Storm

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:06.780
learned the art of stealing on the streets
of Cairo, and even tried to pick Professor

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:07.830
X's pocket.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:12.900
Mystique also gets a new sleeveless white
costume by the end of the movie, in reference

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.900
to her classic white outfits from the comics.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:20.460
At the end of the movie, Mystique is clearly
training the new team in Professor X's Danger

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:21.460
Room.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.770
There's a tease of the classic X-Men robot
foes the Sentinels as they march into the

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:26.090
Danger Room for training.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:30.639
We last saw the Sentinels in Days of Future
Past, but the X-Men also previously trained

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:35.480
against a simulated Sentinel at the beginning
of X-Men: The Last Stand.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:39.110
Although it feels like Jubilee's role was
cut down in the final edit, her character

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:43.390
does get a nice easter egg to her comic book
origin when she visits the shopping mall with

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:45.170
Scott, Jean and Nightcrawler.

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:50.010
In the comics, Jubilee runs away from an orphanage
and hides out in a shopping mall, discovering

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:54.710
her mutant powers while trying to get away
from a security guard.

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:59.020
When everyone wakes up at the X-Mansion during
Jean's nightmare, Professor X tells several

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:03.511
young mutants to return to their rooms, referencing
Mary Poppins when he says "spit-spot, back

00:08:03.511 --> 00:08:04.900
to bed!"

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:09.260
When Charles goes to Moira's office and learns
she has a son, his reaction not only reflects

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:13.111
the shock and disappointment he feels as he
fancies Moira, but it's also a nod to her

00:08:13.111 --> 00:08:14.199
son in the comics.

00:08:14.199 --> 00:08:19.230
In the comics, Moira's son Kevin becomes the
deadly villain Proteus and Bryan Singer has

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:23.050
mentioned that he'd like Proteus to appear
in a future X-Men movie.

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:27.169
The scene also alludes to Charles and Moira's
romantic relationship in the comics and their

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:29.520
kiss at the end of First Class.

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:33.909
When Charles battles Apocalypse, the stress
of using his powers causes him to lose his

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:38.380
hair, which in the comics happens in his teens
again as a result of his mutant powers.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:43.750
And the way Apocalypse grows in size as he
and Charles fight each other is a nod to his

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:47.980
giant size in the comics and how he's able
to change his size there.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:52.260
The opening scene where Apocalypse is trapped
in a collapsing pyramid is an homage to the

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:56.940
1955 movie Land of the Pharaohs, in which
the villain Princess Nellifer played by Joan

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:02.750
Collins is deliberately trapped inside the
Pharaoh's tomb by a huge stone.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:06.650
Magneto's words to Charles "Doesn't it ever
wake you up in the middle of the night, the

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:10.750
feeling that someday, they'll come for you
and your children?" and Charles' reply "I

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:14.830
feel a great swell of pity for the poor soul
that comes to my school looking for trouble"

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:19.080
are almost a direct echo of what Magneto and
Charles said to each other at the very end

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:21.200
of the first X-Men movie.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:25.630
And there's a callback to X-Men 2 when Professor
X reads from TH White's novel, The Once And

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:27.570
Future King, at the X-Mansion.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:31.230
At the end of the second X-Men film, Charles
asked his students if they'd ever read the

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:35.700
book and earlier in the film Magneto was reading
it while he was imprisoned in his plastic

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:36.700
cell.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:40.810
The book also makes a number of appearances
in the X-Men comics, for example, in X-Tinction

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:46.110
Agenda, it's said to be Professor X's favourite
book.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:50.100
Charles actually quotes from The Once And
Future King that "the past must be obliterated

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:51.240
and a new start made.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:55.420
Let us now start fresh without remembrance
rather than live forward and backward at the

00:09:55.420 --> 00:10:00.330
same time", which is an inside joke that pokes
fun at the confusing and often-times inconsistent

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:06.540
timeline of the X-Men movie franchise.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:10.110
There's also a funny moment when Charles says
about Moira that she looks like she's barely

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:15.070
aged a day, which again is the filmmakers
poking fun at the franchise's inconsistencies

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:19.029
as actress Rose Byrne who plays Moira certainly
doesn't look 20 years older than she does

00:10:19.029 --> 00:10:24.060
in First Class, the events of which took place
2 decades before the events of this movie.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:28.700
And there's another joke about the X-Men franchise
when Jubilee, Jean Grey, Cyclops and Nightcrawler

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:32.060
exit the movie theatre having just seen Return
of the Jedi.

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:36.520
When Jean says "we can all agree that the
third one is the worst", it's a joke by writer

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:40.710
Simon Kinberg that The Last Stand which he
also wrote is generally considered to be the

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:43.250
worst movie in the first X-Men trilogy.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:48.750
Bryan Singer is a huge Star Trek fan and building
on the Star Trek reference in Days of Future

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:52.940
Past, the 1967 Star Trek episode "Who Mourns
For Adonais?"

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:56.890
plays in the background on the TV at Storm's
house in Cairo after she brings Apocalypse

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:58.330
back to her home.

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:02.410
The god Apollo who appears in the episode
shares a similarity with Apocalypse, calling

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:06.870
the members of the Enterprise his "children"
and later also growing to a gigantic size

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:09.960
just like Apocalypse does.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:14.040
Actress Ally Sheedy plays Scott Summers' teacher,
who we see educating her students about the

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:18.650
history of the 1973 Paris Peace Accords, in
other words, the events of Days of Future

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:19.650
Past.

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.290
It's fun to see Sheedy playing a teacher,
especially as she orders Scott to see the

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:27.390
School Principal for disrupting the class,
as one of Sheedy's most famous roles is in

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:31.380
director John Hughes' coming-of-age movie
The Breakfast Club where she played compulsive

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:36.250
liar Allison, who spends a whole Saturday
in detention with four other high-school students.

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:41.580
Stan Lee gets a new X-Men cameo, this time
joined by his wife Joan Lee, as they watch

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:46.810
the world's nuclear missiles being fired into
space by Apocalypse.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.580
The post-credits scene includes a nice tease
for the supervillain Mr Sinister.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:55.420
In the scene we see a trio of suited men walking
into the Alkali Lake Weapon X facility where

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.460
they pick up a vial labelled Weapon X DNA.

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:03.470
The vial is then stored in a briefcase labelled
Essex Corp, with Essex referring to Dr Nathanial

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:05.220
Essex from the comics.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:09.790
Essex is a Darwinist geneticist obsessed with
developing a superior species and he's transformed

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:12.580
into Mr Sinister by none other than Apocalypse.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:13.670
In the comics, Mr Sinister has a history of
cloning other mutants and the vial of Weapon

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:20.010
X DNA is a big easter egg to Wolverine's female
clone X-23 appearing in either Wolverine 3

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:21.850
or future X-Men movies.

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:25.420
And if you'd like a more detailed breakdown
of that scene and its implications for the

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:29.300
X-Men movie universe, you can click right
here for my full video on the post-credits.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:33.940
So those were all the easter eggs and references
I spotted in X-Men Apocalypse, but I'm sure

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:38.170
you noticed others too and I'd love to hear
about them in the comments section.

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:42.399
Also make sure you're subscribed for a chance
to win this pack of cool X-Men merch.

